# Component Patterns

## Two component contexts

### `packages/ui/` — reusable components (admin + blog)

- No business logic, no API calls, no TanStack Query hooks, no Redux.
- Receive everything via props — purely presentational or generic UI.
- No `'use client'` unless the primitive itself needs it (e.g. a dropdown with internal state).
- Auto-generated shadcn/ui components live in `packages/ui/src/components/ui/` — never edit manually.

```tsx
// packages/ui/src/components/feedback/LoadingSpinner.tsx
interface ILoadingSpinnerProps {
  size?: 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg';
}

export function LoadingSpinner({ size = 'md' }: ILoadingSpinnerProps): React.JSX.Element {
  return <div className={`animate-spin rounded-full border-2 border-primary size-${size}`} />;
}
```

### `apps/*/components/` — app-specific components

- May contain app logic (TanStack Query hooks, Redux, local state).
- Not shared across apps — if a component from `apps/admin/` becomes useful in `apps/blog/`, move it to `packages/ui/` **without** the business logic.
- `apps/web/components/` never goes to `packages/ui/` — 100% custom landing design.

## Server Components vs Client Components

Next.js App Router defaults to **Server Components**. Only add `'use client'` when strictly necessary.

| Use Server Component | Use Client Component (`'use client'`) |
|---|---|
| SEO pages (blog list, post detail, landing) | Any `useState` / `useEffect` / `useReducer` |
| Static or async rendering | TanStack Query hooks (`useQuery`, `useMutation`) |
| Layouts without interactivity | Redux hooks (`useAppSelector`, `useAppDispatch`) |
| Passing data down as props | Event handlers, forms, browser APIs |
| Environment variables at build time | Auth-gated UI (admin module) |

**Rule**: push `'use client'` as far down the tree as possible. A page can be a Server Component that imports one small Client Component leaf — that is the preferred pattern.

## Component anatomy

```tsx
// apps/blog/src/modules/blog/components/PostCard.tsx
'use client'; // only if needed

import type { IPost } from '../types/blog.types';

interface IPostCardProps {
  post: IPost;
  className?: string;
}

export function PostCard({ post, className }: IPostCardProps): React.JSX.Element {
  return (
    <article className={className}>
      <h2>{post.title}</h2>
      <p>{post.excerpt}</p>
    </article>
  );
}
```

- Always export as a **named export** — no default exports for components.
- Always type props with an interface prefixed `I` (e.g. `IPostCardProps`).
- Always declare the return type explicitly: `React.JSX.Element` or `React.ReactNode`.
- Never put data fetching directly inside a component — delegate to a hook or receive via props.

## Composition rules

- **One component per file.** Co-located sub-components are allowed only when they are never used outside that file, and must be clearly commented.
- **Props over context** for passing data between parent and child. Use context only for truly cross-cutting concerns (theme, auth status).
- **Avoid prop drilling beyond 2 levels.** Lift state or use a hook instead.
- **No anonymous arrow components.** Always use named function declarations or named `const`.

```tsx
// ✅ correct
export function PostList({ posts }: IPostListProps): React.JSX.Element { ... }

// ❌ avoid
export default ({ posts }) => { ... }
```

## Loading and error states

Every Client Component that fetches data must handle three states explicitly:

```tsx
export function PostList(): React.JSX.Element {
  const { data, isLoading, isError } = usePosts();

  if (isLoading) return <LoadingSpinner />;
  if (isError)   return <ErrorState message="No se pudieron cargar los posts." />;
  if (!data?.length) return <EmptyState message="Aún no hay posts publicados." />;

  return (
    <ul>
      {data.map((post) => <PostCard key={post.id} post={post} />)}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

## Page components (`app/` files)

Page files are thin shells. They own the route, set metadata, and compose module components:

```tsx
// apps/blog/src/app/page.tsx
import { PostListContainer } from '@/modules/blog/components/PostListContainer';

export const metadata = { title: 'Blog' };

export default function BlogPage(): React.JSX.Element {
  return <PostListContainer />;
}
```

- Pages never contain JSX beyond layout composition.
- Pages never import axios, Redux, or TanStack Query directly.
